---
title: Proposition 55
---
order: 55
---
short_label: X.55
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If an area is contained by a rational straight line and the second binomial, then the side of the area is the irrational straight line which is called a first bimedial.
----
proof:

Let the area {ABCD|ABCDh} be contained by the rational straight line {AB} and the second binomial {AD}.

I say that the side of the area {AC} is a first bimedial straight line.

Since {AD} is a second binomial straight line, divide it into its terms at {E}, so that {AE} is the greater term. Then {AE} and {ED} are rational straight lines commensurable in square only, the square on {AE} is greater than the square on {ED} by the square on a straight line commensurable with {AE}, and the lesser term {ED} is commensurable in length with {AB}. [!just X.Def.II.2]

